    "content" : "(kxσx + kyσz − sinK kzσy). (12)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 24,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 296.227, 299.083, 329.353 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "This shows that for K ̸= 0,π, the two Weyl nodes have an opposite handedness. To figure out the effect of Sˆ1(k) at low energy, we can also linearize it, and obtain"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 25,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 108.239, 272.842, 299.082, 286.069 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Sˆ1,K(k) = cosK τz + sinK τx. (13)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 26,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 240.51, 299.078, 262.179 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The important feature for K ̸= 0,π is that the second term is non-zero."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 27,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 102.671, 299.086, 241.18 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Thus, together with Sˆ0 = τz, these generate an su(2) algebra acting on the low energy theory. Note that τx acts by exchanging particles at K with holes at −K. Thus, it is convenient to apply a charge conjugation the right-handed Weyl fermion, to give a low energy theory in terms of two left-handed Weyl fermions, now with opposite charge w.r.t. τz rotations. τx rotations meanwhile act by a flavor rotation exchanging the two Weyl fermions. Thus, our symmetry generators Qˆ0, Qˆ1 correspond to two su(2) generators in the flavor symmetry of the low energy, at an angle of K. For K ̸= 0,π, they thus generate the whole chiral symmetry."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 28,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 67.945, 299.085, 100.822 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We can demonstrate that this symmetry protects the gapless Weyl points. To do so, we must break translation symmetry, since otherwise z-axis translations also"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 557.113, 753.375, 562.094, 763.338 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "3"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 29,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 317.014, 704.558, 562.099, 737.435 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "act as a discrete axial symmetry and help to stabilize the Weyl nodes [12, 13]. To analyze translationsymmetry breaking, we consider an extended basis"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 30,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 317.014, 680.537, 562.092, 704.726 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "ek ≡ (ck−K,c†−k+K,ck+K,c†−k−K)T (we suppress the spin component). Hamiltonians in this basis may cou-"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 31,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 317.014, 644.699, 562.096, 680.094 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "ple states at k with k + 2K but are automatically Qˆ0 preserving. In this basis the symmetry action of Qˆ1 becomes"
  }, {
